{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>ships of the Kittim</b> — these are the fleets." "<b>and Kittim</b> — these are the men of Aram." "<b>and he shall be dismayed</b> — this is the king of the north." "<b>and he shall turn back</b> — to his land, in shame." "<b>and he shall be enraged against the holy covenant</b> — this is Agrippas." "<b>and he shall do</b> — a heavy force." "<b>and he shall turn back</b> — to the south." "<b>and he shall give heed to those who forsake the holy covenant</b> — these are the men of Jerusalem, who conspired against the king and killed his men and profaned the Temple, the stronghold that was Israel's, as in גאון עוזך (the glory of your strength), and this word comes by way of the aron ha-berit, which means the aron, aron ha-berit, and likewise הספר המקנה, היין החמה (the book of acquisition, the wine of the heat). Now he recounts the destruction of the Second Temple, and one may wonder at the Sadducean sages, who interpreted this as referring to the future, and said: for the Temple is Mecca, over which the Ishmaelites pilgrimage, and they removed the tamid — the five prayers — and set up the abomination of foreign worship, and these are the erring ones. How is it possible to call only Jerusalem alone 'Temple,' and likewise its name in the Ishmaelite tongue is 'house,' and how is it a holy sanctuary? And do these blind men not open their eyes and see that there is in Mecca an abomination to this day — namely Mercury, to which all Ishmael from east and west pilgrimage to throw stones there? And these interpreters profaned the Temple. And the Gaon [Saadia], of blessed memory, said: 'and he shall do according to his will, the king' — this is the Qadari, head of the kingdom of Ishmael. This is impossible, for it is written 'and he shall not regard the desire of women,' and this one loved women. And they interpreted 'women' as 'provinces'; this is close to derash and is not peshat. Furthermore: what will he do with the written phrase 'those whom his fathers did not know'?! Furthermore: if this were foreign worship, there is no turning away from Mercury, for his fathers knew it and the people of Mecca did not submit to him, until he swore to them that he would not remove the worship of Mercury — and there is no need to elaborate. And Ben ha-Yotzer composed a book on the appointed time of the end, and I have already shattered his words like a potter's vessel before his time arrived, for he hopes for a time that has passed and has included his words among matters of the end. Also the words of R. Solomon ibn Gabirol, of blessed memory: he sought to bind the end to the Great Conjunction of the two upper planets. Also the words of R. Abraham he-Nasi in the book of Qetsim, and the words of ha-Yotzer, and the words of R. Isaac ben Lab, and all who calculate the words or the letters by gematria — all of it is vanity and a striving after wind, for Daniel did not know the end, and how much less those who came after him. Rather, the angel hinted at a matter as I shall explain, but he did not explain when it would be. And now I return to interpret."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:21:24.015251349Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}